  "Süleyman, known in Turkish as Kanuni, 'the Lawgiver', ruled from 1520 to 1566 [[c-suleyman-reign]] (on the Ottoman rise, see 12.7). In 1529 he besieged Vienna, but the siege was lifted. [[c-vienna-1529]] A second Ottoman siege, in 1683, was broken by a relief army under Charles of Lorraine and the Polish king Jan III Sobieski, [[c-vienna-1683]] a defeat Britannica sees as the beginning of the end of Ottoman domination in eastern Europe. [[c-vienna-meaning]] At Smyrna (Izmir) in 1665, Jews acclaimed Sabbatai Zevi as the messiah; his movement spread as far as Amsterdam and London and threatened the authority of the rabbis. [[c-sabbatai-1665]] In 1666, at Adrianople, he converted to Islam. [[c-sabbatai-1666]]",
